AS(1)                          P32W Unix                           AS(1)

NAME
       as - assembler

SYNOPSIS
       as [-o file.o] file.s

DESCRIPTION
       as translates P32W assembly language into a relocatable object
       for ld(1). The output goes to file.o, or to the file named by
       -o; by default it is the source's name without its directory,
       with its suffix replaced by .o (or .o added), in the current
       directory. -c is accepted and ignored (the output is always
       relocatable).

       as reads the whole source, makes two passes over it and
       reports every error it finds as file:line: error: message,
       going on with the next line. If there were errors no object
       is written.

SYNTAX
       A line holds an optional label, then an optional instruction
       or directive, then an optional comment from ; to the end of
       the line:

              loop:   addi  r1, r1, -1      ; count down
                      bnez  r1, loop

       A label is a name followed by a colon. Names are made of
       letters, digits, _ and ., and begin with a letter, _ or .
       Names beginning with .L are local to the file: they never
       reach the symbol table and cannot be external. NAME = expr
       (or .equ NAME, expr) gives a name a value; the expression must
       use only names already defined. A name may be defined only
       once. Mnemonics and directives may be in either case; names
       are case-sensitive. Operands are separated by commas.

       Registers are r0 to r15 (r0 always reads 0; writes to it are
       discarded); zero is r0 and sp is r15. By the C convention r14
       is the frame pointer. Register names are reserved and cannot
       be labels.

       In expressions, numbers are decimal, 0x hex or 0b binary (a
       leading 0 is not octal), and may contain _ for grouping. 'c'
       is a character constant, with the escapes \n \r \t \0 \\ \'
       \". A lone . is the address of the current line. Operators,
       from lowest to highest precedence: |, ^, &, << and >>
       (arithmetic), + and -, * / %; unary - + ~; and ( ).
       Arithmetic is 32-bit.
       hi(x) is bits 31-16 of x and lo(x) bits 15-0.

       A relocatable value is a label or an external name, plus or
       minus a constant. The difference of two labels in the same
       section is a plain number. A relocatable value may appear
       only in .word, in LI and LA, as hi(x) in LUI, as lo(x) in
       ORI, and as the target of JMP or CALL. Any name used but not
       defined (except a .L name) is taken as external, to be
       resolved by ld.

DIRECTIVES
       .text, .data, .bss
              switch to that section; each has its own location
              counter. .bss may hold only .space and .align.
       .global name, ...  (also .globl)
              export names to other objects; each must be defined
              here.
       .extern name, ...
              declare external names (optional).
       .equ name, expr
              same as name = expr.
       .align n
              pad with zeros to a multiple of n (a power of 2).
       .space n
              n zero bytes.
       .word e, ...  .half e, ...
              32- and 16-bit values, little-endian.
       .byte e|"string", ...
              bytes, and the bytes of strings.
       .ascii "s"  .asciz "s"
              a string, without or with a terminating NUL (at most
              1024 bytes).
       .include "file"
              read file (a path relative to the including file's
              directory) in place of this line; nested at most 8
              deep.

INSTRUCTIONS
       Every instruction is one 32-bit word and must be at an address
       that is a multiple of 4. Operand forms: rd, rs, rt registers;
       imm a 16-bit immediate (-32768..32767 signed, 0..65535 for the
       logical ones, 0..31 for shifts); off(rs) a memory operand
       with a signed 16-bit offset ((rs) means 0(rs)); label a
       branch or jump target.

       add sub and or xor nor sll srl sra slt sltu
       mul mulh mulhu div divu rem remu            rd, rs, rt
       addi slti sltiu  andi ori xori  slli srli srai
                                                   rd, rs, imm
       lui                                         rd, imm
       lb lbu lh lhu lw                            rd, off(rs)
       sb sh sw                                    rt, off(rs)
       beq bne blt bge bltu bgeu                   rs, rt, label
       jmp call                                    label
       jr callr                                    rs
       ret syscall break                           (none)

       Branches reach +-32K instructions and must stay in their
       section. JMP and CALL reach +-8M instructions; CALL and CALLR
       push the return address and RET pops it. The privileged
       instructions halt, rte, wait, tlbw, mfcr rd, cr and
       mtcr cr, rs are also assembled (cr is STATUS, EPC, CAUSE,
       BADADDR, VBR, USP, IPEND, IMASK, ASID, TLBHI, TLBLO or a
       number 0-15); in a user program they trap.

       Pseudo-instructions:

       nop                 add r0, r0, r0
       mov rd, rs          add rd, rs, r0
       neg rd, rs          sub rd, r0, rs
       not rd, rs          nor rd, rs, r0
       li rd, expr         one instruction (addi, ori or lui) if the
                           value is known and fits, else lui + ori
       la rd, expr         always lui + ori
       push rs             addi sp, sp, -4; sw rs, 0(sp)
       pop rd              lw rd, 0(sp); addi sp, sp, 4
       bgt bgtu ble bleu a, b, label
                           blt bltu bge bgeu with a and b swapped
       beqz bnez a, label  beq bne a, r0, label

OUTPUT
       The object holds the .text and .data contents, the size of
       .bss, a symbol table of every label (except .L ones) and every
       .global constant, sorted by name, and the relocations: ABS32
       (.word), HI16 (lui), LO16 (ori) and J24 (jmp, call). Its
       format is described in a.out(5). The native as writes byte
       for byte the object the host assembler (p32asm.py -c) writes.

EXAMPLES
       as hello.s                    makes hello.o
       as -o /tmp/x.o x.s

       A complete program, linked with ld /usr/lib/crt0.o hello.o
       /usr/lib/libc.a:

                      .text
                      .global main
              main:   la    r1, msg
                      call  puts
                      li    r1, 0
                      ret
                      .data
              msg:    .asciz "hello"

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success; 1 if the source cannot be read, has errors, or
       the object cannot be written; 2 for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       cc(1), ld(1), ar(1), db(1), a.out(5)

BUGS
       Only one source file is assembled at a time: naming several is
       a usage error. There is no listing and no macro facility; use
       cc -E on a .c file if you need a preprocessor.

       .org is refused: only relocatable objects are made (the host
       assembler also makes flat ROM images). Arithmetic is modulo
       2^32, so some out-of-range values the host assembler refuses
       are accepted.

       Strings know only the escapes listed above (no \x or octal).
       A load or store offset cannot be relocatable: LA the address
       first.
